---
title: Local results and CI verdicts
intent: Write machine-readable results and turn them into a local pass/fail verdict.
group: automation
features:
- ci
- reporting
command: curlew run testapi/collections/00-smoke.yaml --var mud="$MUDFLAT_URL" --var ws="$MUDFLAT_WS_URL" --var run="$RUN_ID"
order: 120
runnable: true
---

The CI runner can archive the JSON, JUnit or HTML artifact. Curlew does not upload it or post a pull-request check itself. Your CI system reports the process status. Private-repository installation requires authenticated GitHub access.

## Prerequisites

Use the complete repository checkout and start Mudflat as described in the [cookbook setup](https://github.com/weiqigod/curlew/blob/main/site/README.md). Run these commands from the repository root with `curlew` on PATH.

```bash
export MUDFLAT_URL="${MUDFLAT_URL:-http://127.0.0.1:18080}"
export MUDFLAT_WS_URL="${MUDFLAT_WS_URL:-ws://127.0.0.1:18080}"
export RUN_ID="cookbook-$(date +%s)-$$"
```

## Run it

```bash
curlew run testapi/collections/00-smoke.yaml --var mud="$MUDFLAT_URL" --var ws="$MUDFLAT_WS_URL" --var run="$RUN_ID" --format json > results.json
curlew pr-check --results results.json --summary verdict.json
```

A successful execution exits 0. Read assertion outcomes as well as the exit code.
No example output or timing numbers are fabricated here.

## Complete collection

<!-- cookbook-source: testapi/collections/00-smoke.yaml -->
```yaml
# The server is up, describes itself, and round-trips a request.
#
# If this collection fails, nothing else in the suite is worth reading.
name: Mudflat smoke
description: |
  Proves mudflat is running and that curlew can reach it. Every later
  collection assumes this one passes.

requests:
  - name: index lists endpoints
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      headers:
        Content-Type: { equals: "application/json" }
      body:
        $.name: { equals: "mudflat" }
        $.endpoints: { type: array }
      cel:
        - "response.body.endpoints.size() > 20"

  - name: capabilities reports the phase and what is absent
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/capabilities"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        $.phase: { equals: 4 }
        $.families: { type: array }
        # What is absent is reported so a caller can tell a missing capability
        # from a broken one.
        $.Absent: { exists: true }

  - name: echo round-trips a request
    request:
      method: POST
      url: "{{mud}}/echo"
      headers:
        X-Dogfood: "smoke"
      query:
        k: "v"
      body:
        hello: "mudflat"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        $.request.method: { equals: "POST" }
        $.server.name: { equals: "mudflat" }
        $.request.body_len: { greater_than: 0 }
        # The envelope reports headers as ordered pairs, so a header curlew
        # claims to send either appears here or it was never sent.
        $.request.header_names_in_order: { contains: "X-Dogfood" }
        $.request.query: { length: 1 }

  - name: echo reports the session from the path
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/s/{{run}}-smoke/echo"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        $.server.session: { exists: true }
        $.connection.id: { exists: true }

  - name: anything accepts any path depth
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/anything/deeply/nested/path/segments"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        $.request.target: { contains: "deeply/nested" }

  - name: empty body is distinguishable from a missing one
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/empty"
    assertions:
      status: 200

  - name: session reset is idempotent
    request:
      method: DELETE
      url: "{{mud}}/s/{{run}}-smoke"
    assertions:
      status: 204

  - name: unknown path is a clean 404
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/no-such-endpoint-exists"
    assertions:
      status: 404

  - name: the OpenAPI document is served
    description: |
      The §9.P round trip runs in testapi/harness/openapi.sh — importing a
      document and running the result is a command, not a response, so no
      collection can express it. What a collection can check is that the
      document is here and is the version-controlled one.
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/openapi.json"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      headers:
        Content-Type: { equals: "application/json" }
      body:
        $.openapi: { equals: "3.1.0" }
        $.info.title: { equals: "Mudflat" }
        # Hand-written, not generated from the handlers: a generated document
        # would make the round trip a tautology.
        $.info.description: { contains: "HAND-WRITTEN" }
        $.paths./echo.get.operationId: { equals: "echoGet" }
```
